MAYA: Iran just warned ships that transiting the Strait of Hormuz without their approval is unacceptable and dangerous. Which is funny because Iran's basically saying 'you need our permission slip to use this waterway,' and they're delivering it like a parent who just discovered their kid's been sneaking out.

12
00:02:26,110 --> 00:02:35,516
JAMIE: So Iran's not just threatening ships, they're establishing a permission structure for one of the most critical shipping lanes on Earth. That's not a warning, that's a business model.

13
00:02:35,516 --> 00:02:44,609
JAMIE: On the tape tomorrow: we've got a cluster of earnings and some macro data that'll either confirm the rotation is real or send everyone back to panic-buying Nvidia.

14
00:02:44,609 --> 00:03:03,108
ALEX: Darden reports again on June twenty-fifth, which means we get a second look at whether casual dining traffic is actually stabilizing or if that last print was just a dead-cat bounce. Same day you've got Commercial Metals and T D Synnex—both cyclical plays that'll tell you whether the capex cycle is still alive or if we're actually slowing.

15
00:03:03,108 --> 00:03:19,725
MAYA: And BlackBerry's reporting the same day, which is wild because nobody remembers BlackBerry is still a company. They pivoted to software and cybersecurity years ago, so if they beat, it's proof that the old phone empire actually found a second act. If they miss, it's just another zombie that forgot to die.

16
00:03:19,725 --> 00:03:31,640
JAMIE: So basically we get to watch whether the shovels are still selling, whether people are still eating out, and whether a company that used to be synonymous with 'outdated' can actually execute. That's the frame for tomorrow.

17
